; exit 76 (see .exit)
; `MM-ALLOC-16b`, enforced. An evidence record is an ordinary arena
; object with no protection from reclamation, so a program that resets
; a mark taken BEFORE a `handle` whose extent is still live frees the
; record the slot still points at - and the next operation dispatches
; through it. The rule said a program MUST NOT, and nothing said so
; when one did.
;
; Measured on the compiler at `ac169e1`, on the third block below,
; before the check existed:
;
;     dispatched through a freed evidence record
;     exit 0
;
; - the operation ran, off memory the same call had reclaimed, and the
; program exited successfully. It traps now, with status 76 and a
; sentence, and `MM-ALLOC-16b` is an implementation obligation rather
; than a program one.
;
; THE FIRST TWO BLOCKS ARE THE HALF THAT MUST NOT MOVE. A trap is only
; worth having if it fires on nothing else, and the two legal shapes
; either side of this one are easy to break by accident:
;
;   - a mark taken INSIDE the extent and reset inside it. The record
;     was allocated before the mark, so it sits below the waterline
;     and the reset cannot reach it. This is the ordinary use.
;   - a mark and reset with no handle in scope at all, which is every
;     arena program in the tree today.
;
; NEITHER DOES THE RECOVERY PATH TRAP, and it is the one that looked
; like it would. `__axiom_recover_abort` IS an arena reset across a
; live extent - that is its whole job - but `emitRecoverRuntime` calls
; `@__axiom_recover_load` BEFORE `@__axiom_arena_reset_fn`, so every
; slot has already been overwritten with its arm-time value by the
; time the check runs. The abort arranges for the test to be false on
; its own, with no exemption and nothing to distinguish.
; `tests/stdlib/401-recover-effect.ax` is that path and still exits 71.
;
; The status is in `.exit`, the sentence in `.err`, and neither checks
; the other: a program that printed the right sentence and exited 0
; fails on the status, and one that exited 76 in silence fails on the
; stderr.
(import IO)

(import Mem)

(effect Console
  (log :: (-> String Int)))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    ; --- LEGAL: the mark is taken inside the extent ---
    (handle {
      (let ((m __axiom_arena_mark))
        {
          (memAlloc 64)
          (__axiom_arena_reset m)
          (log "inner mark, reset inside the extent")
        }
      )
      0
    } (Console IO Alloc Mut) (lambda (s) { (println s) 0 }))
    ; --- LEGAL: no handle in scope at all ---
    (let ((m2 __axiom_arena_mark))
      {
        (memAlloc 64)
        (__axiom_arena_reset m2)
        (println "no handle in scope")
      }
    )
    ; --- ILLEGAL: the mark predates the extent ---
    ;
    ; `m3` is taken before the `handle`, so the evidence record the
    ; handler installs sits ABOVE the waterline this reset restores.
    ; The reset reclaims it and the `log` below would dispatch through
    ; it. This is where the process ends.
    (let ((m3 __axiom_arena_mark))
      (handle {
        (__axiom_arena_reset m3)
        (log "dispatched through a freed evidence record")
        0
      } (Console IO Alloc Mut) (lambda (s) { (println s) 0 }))
    )
    ; NOT REACHED, and deliberately not a bare numeral: this program's
    ; status comes from the trap rather than from its last expression,
    ; and this line is the tripwire for the check being removed - if a
    ; reset past a live handle is ever accepted again, this runs and
    ; the golden says so.
    (printlnLit (strCStr "UNREACHABLE: the reset past a live handle was accepted"))
  }
)
